            "typeName": "dsDescription",
            "multiple": true,
            "typeClass": "compound",
            "value": [
              {
                "dsDescriptionValue": {
                  "typeName": "dsDescriptionValue",
                  "multiple": false,
                  "typeClass": "primitive",
                  "value": "Optic flow can significantly influence the perception and exertion of effort. In \nthis study, we investigated the effects of exposure to proximal and distal areas \nof the optic flow field on exerted and perceived cycling effort. Thirty cyclists participated in two 20-min cycling trials within a virtual reality environment, with \nthe goal of surpassing the power output achieved during a baseline trial. During \nthese trials, they viewed the environment through a proximal or distal window, \nin counterbalanced order. We measured the cyclists' exerted effort on a bicycle \ntrainer, and recorded their responses regarding perceived effort and psychological momentum. A one-way repeated measures ANCOVA with average baseline \npower as a covariate revealed a significant difference in exerted effort between \nthe proximal and distal condition, with higher average exerted effort in the proximal condition. However, a significant interaction effect between condition and \nbaseline power indicated that the beneficial effect of the proximal condition was \nmainly present for lower-level cyclists. We observed no significant differences \nin perceived effort or psychological momentum. These findings provide novel \ninsights into the relation between optic flow and cycling effort, and call for new \nresearch on the mechanisms underlying this relation."
                },
                "dsDescriptionDate": {
                  "typeName": "dsDescriptionDate",
                  "multiple": false,
